# Syntax corpus — tracker tie-spread roll (E152, migrated to E153 syntax).
#
# Tie (`_` — was `~` before ADR 0077) and `*N` repeat marker exist in the
# grammar; epic E152 introduced the row-build interpretation that absorbs
# a tie following a `*N` anchor into the roll's spread schedule. ADR
# 0077 (ticket 0946) renames the tie token from `~` to `_` and keeps the
# absorption behaviour exactly as before. Per syntax-corpus policy
# (memory: feedback_syntax_corpus_policy), any change to a syntactic
# construct's parse shape gets a corpus entry so future grammar work
# does not silently regress the annotation this depends on.
#
# Behavioural assertions live in patches-core / patches-interpreter /
# patches-modules tests; this file only locks in parse-acceptance
# shape. The `|` row-continuation case below verifies that the parser
# merges continuation steps into one Vec<Step> before row-build, so a
# `*N _` span can straddle the join.
#
# Each body ends with an empty `patch {}` because the file grammar
# requires a trailing patch block. The auto-wrap path skips bodies
# starting with `pattern `.

=== ok plain repeat anchor without ties
pattern p {
    kick: x*3
}
patch {}

=== ok repeat anchor followed by one tie (span 2)
pattern p {
    kick: x*3 _
}
patch {}

=== ok repeat anchor followed by two ties (span 3)
pattern p {
    kick: x*5 _ _
}
patch {}

=== ok chained anchors interleaved with ties
pattern p {
    snare: x*3 _ _ _ x*2 _ .
}
patch {}

=== ok plain tie after a non-repeat note keeps sustain shape
pattern p {
    vox: A3 _ _
}
patch {}

=== ok tie-spread across the row-continuation pipe
pattern p {
    kick: x*3
        | _ _
}
patch {}

=== ok multi-channel mix of sustain and spread ties
pattern p {
    kick:  x*3 _      x*5 _ _     x*3
    snare: .   x _    .   .   x*2 _
}
patch {}

=== ok repeat anchor with cv2 plus slide plus tie
pattern p {
    bass: A3:0.5> /B3 x*3 _
}
patch {}
